# Monitor types {% #monitor-types %}

| Type | What it checks | Where to use it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Usage](/docs/products/monitors/usage) | A project or resource metric, such as outbound bandwidth, database reads, or storage used. | Appwrite Cloud and self-hosted Appwrite |
| [HTTP](/docs/products/monitors/endpoint-checks#http) | A URL's response status, response time, and HTTPS certificate expiry. | Paid Appwrite Cloud plans |
| [DNS](/docs/products/monitors/endpoint-checks#dns) | Whether a domain resolves and returns the expected answer. | Paid Appwrite Cloud plans |
| [TCP](/docs/products/monitors/endpoint-checks#tcp) | Whether a host accepts a connection on a port. | Paid Appwrite Cloud plans |
| [SMTP](/docs/products/monitors/endpoint-checks#smtp) | Whether a mail server accepts an SMTP session and completes the configured TLS handshake. | Paid Appwrite Cloud plans |

# How it works {% #how-it-works %}

1. Create a monitor and define the condition or failed check that should open an alert.
2. Choose a severity and who should be notified. You can notify organization roles or use an escalation to notify people in steps.
3. Use the **Monitors** tab to manage checks and see their latest results. Use the **Alerts** tab to review firing and resolved alerts, and acknowledge them.

Each alert is also a project event, so [webhooks and functions](/docs/products/monitors/alerts#use-project-events) can post it to chat, pause a runaway function, or log an incident. You can manage monitors and alerts with the server SDKs too.

# Plan limits {% #plan-limits %}

On Appwrite Cloud, the plan sets how many monitors you can create and how often they run:

| | Free | Pro |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Usage monitors | 3 per project, checked every 15 minutes | 25 per project, checked every minute |
| Synthetic checks (HTTP, DNS, TCP, SMTP) | Not available | 10 per organization, then $2 per check per month, as often as every minute |
| Escalations | 1, with up to 2 steps | 10, with up to 10 steps each |
| On-call responders | 1 | 3 included, then $25 per responder per month |

On Pro, the **Create monitor** page shows how many synthetic checks the organization uses and what an additional one costs. It tells you when you reach a limit. See [Pricing](/pricing) for Enterprise limits.

